        "Greater Noida's spacious apartment layouts have made home cinema rooms with dedicated home theatre recliner sets a genuine mainstream feature in mid-to-upper residential developments — something that remains aspirational in most Delhi NCR markets. These multi-seat recliner systems — typically 4-seat or 7-seat configurations from brands like Mstar, Evok, HomeCity, or imported from South Korean and Chinese manufacturers — are engineered differently from single-seat recliners, and their maintenance needs reflect that engineering complexity. Each seat typically has its own independent motor, but all seats share a single transformer and control bus, which means a failure in the shared wiring harness can disable the entire set while each individual seat motor remains perfectly functional. Diagnosing home theatre recliner faults correctly requires understanding this shared-architecture design — a distinction that trips up many general repairers who test individual motors in isolation and declare them 'fine' when the system fault lies upstream.",
        "Cup holder assemblies in home theatre recliners are a surprisingly common repair request. The integrated heated/cooled cup holders found in premium sets use a small Peltier thermoelectric module — the same technology used in compact car fridges — driven by a 12V supply tapped from the main transformer. The Peltier module itself rarely fails, but the condensation it produces (on the cooling cycle) is frequently allowed to track back along the supply wiring, causing corrosion at the connector block that leads to intermittent or complete cup holder failure. The correct fix is not simply to replace the Peltier module but to clean and re-seal all connectors, apply dielectric grease, and route the drainage channel to prevent future tracking. In many Delhi NCR homes, this maintenance step is completely overlooked, and cup holders that could be repaired for ₹500 are instead sealed with tape and abandoned.",
        "USB charging port failures are the second most frequent maintenance issue in Greater Noida home theatre recliners. The 5V USB modules embedded in armrest panels are low-cost components typically rated for 2–3 amps, but they are routinely overloaded by users simultaneously charging tablets, smartphones, and wireless earphone cases. Most homeowners are surprised to learn that the USB port itself rarely fails — rather, the small inline fuse protecting the port's 5V supply line blows to prevent overload damage. Replacing the fuse (a ₹20–₹50 component) restores the port immediately. However, if the fuse has blown repeatedly, the correct response is to fit a slightly higher-rated fuse and investigate whether a higher-current USB module would be appropriate — not to simply keep replacing the same fuse until the module board burns.",
